    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `run_tool` in `opsbot/calls.py`. The 3 existing tests pass, but none of them call `run_tool`, and I couldn't run a live server check. So far it's only been checked against the installed library's source.\n\nThe version pinned here (`mcp==2.2.0`) is newer than what I know, so I read the installed library's source instead of relying on docs. On this version, `session.call_tool()` returns a `CallToolResult` with `is_error`, `content` and `structured_content`.\n\n1. **Call failed:** if `result.is_error` is set, it raises `ToolCallFailed(name, message)`, where `message` is the text blocks joined with `\"\\n\"`.\n2. **Structured output:** otherwise, if the result has structured output, it's returned as-is.\n3. **Text:** otherwise it returns the text blocks joined with `\"\\n\"`.\n\nChoices worth knowing:\n- **Absence check:** it checks `structured_content is not None` rather than whether the value is truthy. On the newest protocol version, structured output can be any JSON value, so `0`, `[]` or `\"\"` are still returned correctly. The one exception: if a server explicitly sends `null` as structured output, you get the text instead, because that looks the same as no structured output.\n- **No new dependency:** text blocks are picked out by their `type == \"text\"` field. That avoids importing the `mcp_types` package directly, which is installed only as a dependency of `mcp` and isn't listed in `pyproject.toml`."
  },
